{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe supports probable common ancestry of these domains alongside functional divergence. In release 2.08, they are three distinct families: alcohol dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains, c.2.1.1 (SUNID 51736); CoA-binding domains, c.2.1.8 (51900); and potassium-channel NAD-binding domains, c.2.1.9 (63944). All share the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold superfamily c.2.1 (51735), within fold c.2 (51734) and the alpha/beta class c. Their shared superfamily provides the evolutionary inference; shared fold or class alone would establish only structural similarity. Family membership represents a closer relationship than this cross-family connection.

Biologically, this is consistent with an ancestral nucleotide-binding scaffold adapted to different ligand interactions and protein contexts. It does not establish identical chemistry. Alcohol-dehydrogenase activity cannot be transferred to the CoA-binding or potassium-channel families; CoA specificity cannot be transferred to the others; and channel regulation cannot be assigned to an enzyme domain. Neither the superfamily name nor the potassium-channel family name establishes NAD versus NADP preference, actual ligand occupancy, or catalytic activity in every member. The relationship concerns the classified domains, not necessarily their entire proteins. SCOPe specifically annotates the alcohol-dehydrogenase-like family with “N-terminal all-beta domain defines family,” emphasizing that its family interpretation also depends on domain context.

Stronger functional assignments require sequence and active-site comparisons, ligand-bound structures, biochemical binding and activity measurements, and full domain-architecture evidence; channel-regulatory claims additionally need functional evidence in the channel context. Structural alignment is needed before treating one family as a modeling template for another, especially for binding loops and interfaces. The retrieved hierarchy does not provide those experiments or alignments, nor establish a detailed ancestral function or direction of divergence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=potassium&mode=auto&limit=15","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=CoA&mode=auto&limit=15","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/children?limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/homology"]}]}
